It only got, you know, probably a dozen sales totally.

237
00:12:48,960 --> 00:12:56,960
But it was definitely good to have a project that gave me a goal to work towards so I could learn the framework and learn everything involved.

238
00:12:57,360 --> 00:13:02,320
And take it all the way through to the store and have real customers even if there wasn't very many of them.

239
00:13:02,640 --> 00:13:03,280
Yeah, yeah.

240
00:13:03,520 --> 00:13:06,320
I mean, you know, my mom emailing for customer support was great.

241
00:13:06,720 --> 00:13:07,920
That was an easy one to answer.

242
00:13:08,560 --> 00:13:12,160
She was probably one of the only sales belligerent, but, you know.

243
00:13:12,320 --> 00:13:12,400
Yeah.

244
00:13:12,480 --> 00:13:15,280
Yeah, she didn't know what to do with that app at all.

245
00:13:17,360 --> 00:13:23,200
But yeah, so that was, I think, my first quote unquote app that wasn't just a little hacky project.

246
00:13:24,000 --> 00:13:27,280
But then it really just kind of sat on everything until slopes.

247
00:13:27,600 --> 00:13:29,200
Okay, so let's get into the slopes then.

248
00:13:29,920 --> 00:13:32,480
I'll let you just give the quick pitch for what it is.

249
00:13:33,040 --> 00:13:39,200
So I usually say, it's, you know, think Nike plus Runkeeper, Strava, all those GPS tracking apps.

250
00:13:39,680 --> 00:13:42,560
They're usually targeted towards runners or people on bikes.

251
00:13:43,280 --> 00:13:49,440
But slopes is that for skiers and snowboarders because we have some very specific aspects to it our niche.

252
00:13:49,840 --> 00:13:53,360
For example, we take lifts up the hill and then we go downhill.

253
00:13:53,920 --> 00:14:02,640
And when we're looking at statistics, we don't want that uphill travel to count towards, you know, our total distance or our average speed or anything like that.

254
00:14:03,440 --> 00:14:05,680
So we want that filtered out or at least separated.

255
00:14:05,840 --> 00:14:10,640
Versus a running app, you would have to hit pause every time you get on the lift before you would go up.

256
00:14:10,720 --> 00:14:14,000
And then you'd have to hit start going down if you want to filter that kind of stuff.

257
00:14:14,640 --> 00:14:17,520
So it's a lot more, you know, specialized than that.

258
00:14:17,920 --> 00:14:27,440
But that's kind of the reason that apps very specific to this niche exist is because we do have a very specific set of requirements for what kind of data we care about.

259
00:14:27,760 --> 00:14:27,840
Okay.

260
00:14:28,480 --> 00:14:32,400
And it runs on your phone, you can put it in your pocket, and then you have a watch app as well, right?

261
00:14:32,720 --> 00:14:32,800
Yeah.

262
00:14:33,200 --> 00:14:33,280
Yeah.

263
00:14:34,080 --> 00:14:34,240
Cool.

264
00:14:34,800 --> 00:14:38,720
So how did that actually get started as a product then?

265
00:14:39,280 --> 00:14:46,800
So I guess like most developers, they use another app and they say, I can do a better job than this.

266
00:14:47,520 --> 00:14:51,120
And then they commit to the next eight years of their life of doing a better job than that.

267
00:14:52,160 --> 00:14:56,720
It turns out it's a lot of work when you say that, but in this case it paid off.

268
00:14:57,920 --> 00:14:59,040
Yeah, I was using another app.

269
00:14:59,360 --> 00:15:00,000
It was well engineered.

270
00:15:00,800 --> 00:15:08,160
My friends and I were using it on our phones back in like 2012 and it worked great, but the UI was definitely written by an engineer.

271
00:15:08,560 --> 00:15:10,000
And I was just like, you know what?

272
00:15:10,160 --> 00:15:15,120
I'm having to poke around too many screens to figure out like, hey, where was my top speed for the day?

273
00:15:15,520 --> 00:15:16,960
I want to know where that was on the mountain.

274
00:15:17,200 --> 00:15:17,840
Pretty simple question.

275
00:15:18,000 --> 00:15:25,600
I had to hop between like three or four screens and cross reference and it just wasn't laid out the way that skiers and snowboarders think, I think.

276
00:15:26,720 --> 00:15:30,880
So at that point, I was like, okay, I can do a better job than that.

277
00:15:31,520 --> 00:15:35,520
But it really, I sat on it for geez, I don't know.

278
00:15:36,560 --> 00:15:40,640
Eight months, for some time, I just sat on it because I'm like, you know what?

279
00:15:40,880 --> 00:15:41,360
There's no way.

280
00:15:41,840 --> 00:15:44,000
I just didn't have the confidence to move forward with it.

281
00:15:44,240 --> 00:15:48,160
It was something I'm like, you know, okay, I might be able to make something here.

282
00:15:49,760 --> 00:15:55,280
First doubting myself if I could do a good enough job to actually do better, but then also doubting like, is this worth the effort?

283
00:15:55,440 --> 00:15:56,320
Is there a market here?

284
00:15:57,200 --> 00:16:08,720
And so I sat on it for quite some time until there was a Philly Startup weekend, which is like a 48 hour hackathon where people can go and kind of present their ideas and then people can vote what they want to work on.

285
00:16:09,520 --> 00:16:15,600
And at the end of the weekend, they'll just kind of bring in some mentors and kind of like do a little judging of the products and stuff like that.

286
00:16:16,160 --> 00:16:20,560
And I presented Slopes, a little 30 seconds spiel and boy, I botched it.

287
00:16:20,720 --> 00:16:21,200
It bombed.

288
00:16:21,840 --> 00:16:25,040
I mean, I think I did okay, but I wasn't good at selling it.

289
00:16:25,520 --> 00:16:27,120
So nobody voted for Slopes.

290
00:16:27,920 --> 00:16:36,400
So I was, you know, Friday night kind of dejected and stuff, but then I ended up with a group of people that none of us really felt good about the other products that were out there per se.

291
00:16:37,120 --> 00:16:39,520
At least we weren't drawn to the ones that people were working on.

292
00:16:40,160 --> 00:16:45,680
So we just kind of sat down and said, well, between all the products that we had pitched, which one do we want to work on?

293
00:16:46,160 --> 00:16:48,800
And Slopes ended up being the one that we wanted to work on.

294
00:16:49,520 --> 00:16:56,000
So I had come that weekend with a quick prototype of some of the GPS processing that needed to be proof of concept to make sure I could do this.

295
00:16:56,880 --> 00:17:06,000
And we spent the weekend just kind of, you know, looking into the market, doing some research, figuring out, you know, taking some of my design ideas and actually putting them on paper so we could show them.

296
00:17:06,640 --> 00:17:08,720
And then it ended winning the startup weekend.

297
00:17:08,960 --> 00:17:09,280
Oh man.

298
00:17:10,000 --> 00:17:22,960
Yeah, from no votes at all to, yeah, it won first prize, which was, I think, a large pack of Red Bull and some books.

299
00:17:24,400 --> 00:17:24,480
Yeah.

300
00:17:24,720 --> 00:17:26,480
Obviously, it made a big impact on you.

301
00:17:26,800 --> 00:17:28,000
The rest of the day did.

302
00:17:28,080 --> 00:17:28,160
Yeah.

303
00:17:28,240 --> 00:17:28,320
Yeah.

304
00:17:29,120 --> 00:17:34,160
Um, yeah, I have a picture somewhere and we got a stupid like Burger King crown, basically.

305
00:17:34,560 --> 00:17:42,960
Um, but, but that was, although nothing massive came out of that weekend, um, it isn't like, suddenly I had a product or something.

306
00:17:43,360 --> 00:17:58,640
Like, I think that just gave me kind of the confidence I needed that, like, oh, look, people who have some, you know, foot in the door in terms of building products and stuff like that, uh, looked at this and said that they actually think there might be something here.

307
00:17:58,960 --> 00:18:09,120
You know, they were actually impressed with some of the takes I was trying to do on how I had approached design differently and some of the new things I wanted to do because I thought there were some interesting experiments to do here.

308
00:18:09,440 --> 00:18:11,760
And they actually thought it was worthwhile.

309
00:18:12,320 --> 00:18:18,960
So at that point that was kind of the, I think, the confidence booster I needed to be like, okay, you know what, I'll give this a chance.

310
00:18:19,440 --> 00:18:21,520
I'll give this a real chance and see what happens.

311
00:18:22,320 --> 00:18:35,520
And so it seems like kind of the big differentiator then in those early days, the idea, the sort of idea, so to speak, is a different way to visualize the same data that most of the other apps were capturing.

312
00:18:36,000 --> 00:18:36,000
Yeah.

313
00:18:36,240 --> 00:18:41,360
Yeah, at the time there was probably two main apps at the time, one of which is still around today.

314
00:18:41,840 --> 00:18:45,200
They're my 99 cent competitor, the one that I had used beforehand.

315
00:18:47,120 --> 00:18:50,560
And the other one was a bit more social.

316
00:18:50,960 --> 00:18:53,520
I think even back then they had leaderboards and stuff like that.

317
00:18:54,800 --> 00:18:57,040
So they both did an okay job tracking.

318
00:18:58,400 --> 00:19:06,080
The 99 cent one, anyone who has seen me talk on Twitter about business models and stuff, you know my opinion of the sustainability of that.

319
00:19:07,600 --> 00:19:13,360
The other one, they had always struggled to monetize and I think they didn't quite do their pa payroll right.

320
00:19:14,160 --> 00:19:17,520
So they were kind of the free app, but they were struggling to stay in business.

321
00:19:18,080 --> 00:19:20,640
And they actually haven't updated their app now in three years.

322
00:19:21,200 --> 00:19:24,160
They pivoted to soccer, which is actually really interesting.

323
00:19:24,720 --> 00:19:27,520
And they're doing a amazing job there, but that's a whole different thing.

324
00:19:28,480 --> 00:19:31,120
I need to look that up, because I'm just curious what that even looks like.

325
00:19:31,680 --> 00:19:35,120
Well, they used to be called Alpine Replay, and now they're called Trace.

326
00:19:36,000 --> 00:19:36,960
So if you want to look it up.

327
00:19:37,760 --> 00:19:46,000
So the interesting quick story of them is, so they were doing iPhone tracking for skiing and snowboarding, but then they were struggling to do any kind of monetization.

328
00:19:46,320 --> 00:19:48,000
It just wasn't really working out too well.

329
00:19:48,720 --> 00:19:51,600
I think they were giving way too much away for free is what it came down to.

330
00:19:52,240 --> 00:19:54,960
And so they decided, okay, well, let's get into the hardware business.

331
00:19:55,600 --> 00:19:58,560
Skiers and snowboarders, they pay for hardware, so let's give that a shot.

332
00:19:59,040 --> 00:20:14,160
So they started building this little tracking puck that you could put on your skis or snowboard that would that had better GPS in it and had accelerometer data in it and would connect via Bluetooth to your phone.

333
00:20:14,400 --> 00:20:18,240
So the puck would do all the data collection and then your phone would just process it.

334
00:20:18,720 --> 00:20:22,000
And that got you much higher resolution data than you could get with your phone.

335
00:20:22,320 --> 00:20:22,480
Right.

336
00:20:22,880 --> 00:20:23,840
And much more reliable data.

337
00:20:24,400 --> 00:20:31,200
Because with an accelerometer on your ski, you know, okay, this person just did a backside 360 or something off that job.

338
00:20:32,240 --> 00:20:35,680
You can't tell that when the phone's in your pocket because your phone's bouncing around like crazy.

339
00:20:36,000 --> 00:20:36,160
Right.

340
00:20:36,560 --> 00:20:38,880
So they were selling this device for 200 bucks.

341
00:20:39,040 --> 00:20:44,800
Their Kickstarter campaign did really well, and they also marketed it for surfboards, which makes a lot of sense.

342
00:20:45,520 --> 00:20:50,800
So you go out surfing and then you could come back and sync it back to your phone later, but you wouldn't have to take your phone out in the ocean.

343
00:20:51,600 --> 00:20:59,200
So they did that good Kickstarter, but I don't think it really met expectations in terms of long term sales for them.

344
00:20:59,840 --> 00:21:05,920
But that got them some hardware expertise in doing this kind of accelerometer GPS data type stuff.

345
00:21:06,720 --> 00:21:18,480
So then they pivoted that over to like this little thing that you could attach to your soccer boot that would track your movements and like how you would kick and all this kind of stuff while you're playing soccer.

346
00:21:19,200 --> 00:21:24,880
And then they took that and they also built on top of that this like camera system.

347
00:21:25,200 --> 00:21:27,840
And I'm not an expert of their product, so I might be botching a little bit.

348
00:21:28,560 --> 00:21:33,840
But from what I've been able to gather from their website, like you can buy this camera system from them and set it up in the stands.

349
00:21:34,400 --> 00:21:48,800
And then it can automatically just based on the GPS and everything, record everyone who's going, identify who is where and automatically create highlights of a certain player's movements during that time.

350
00:21:49,120 --> 00:21:54,640
Like you can look at all the data or it can automatically figure out like, oh, here's where they were doing some kicks or something like that.

351
00:21:56,000 --> 00:22:00,480
And coaches can use it and you can use it for like a highlight reel and all that kind of stuff.

352
00:22:00,640 --> 00:22:08,240
And they're licensing this to teams as a way for coaches to improve what they're doing, their training and like showing all that kind of stuff.

353
00:22:08,400 --> 00:22:13,920
Yeah, because like taking like recording tapes and reviewing those and all that is like a big thing thing with sports.

354
00:22:14,640 --> 00:22:14,800
Right.

355
00:22:15,360 --> 00:22:18,800
And like we've seen iPhone apps that do this, but now it's automatic.

356
00:22:19,120 --> 00:22:24,560
They just have the camera in the stands and it's tracking all of the players and able to pull that stuff out automatically.

357
00:22:24,880 --> 00:22:27,360
And that saves a lot of burden on the coaches.

358
00:22:28,160 --> 00:22:33,120
So they're all in over there in that soccer area now, and they seem to be doing a bang up job.

359
00:22:33,280 --> 00:22:38,320
Like they've figured out, I think, what works in that space.

360
00:22:38,880 --> 00:22:40,480
And they're like scaling up employees.

361
00:22:40,720 --> 00:22:44,480
Like they seem to be getting a lot of traction that they never got in the ski and snowboard space.

362
00:22:44,640 --> 00:22:45,680
So kudos to them.

363
00:22:46,480 --> 00:22:46,560
Yeah.

364
00:22:46,880 --> 00:22:47,680
No, that's fascinating.

365
00:22:47,920 --> 00:22:48,000
Yeah.

366
00:22:48,080 --> 00:22:51,680
Yeah, it's I was, the first time I saw him do soccer, I'm like, what?

367
00:22:53,200 --> 00:22:56,400
But looking at him now, they're kicking butt, so it's really cool to see.

368
00:22:56,960 --> 00:22:58,960
But yeah, they were the other competitor I had.

369
00:23:00,240 --> 00:23:18,720
So seguing that tangent back to the original, it so what I guess I was a little bit cocky and, you know, I was thinking like, oh okay, well, you know, my differentiator will be I'll have a better design, you know, very Apple, very, you know, full of myself.

370
00:23:18,880 --> 00:23:20,000
I'm going to do this great design.

371
00:23:20,400 --> 00:23:22,400
It's going to be so intuitive, all that kind of stuff.

372
00:23:22,480 --> 00:23:24,160
And looking back, boy, was it bad.

373
00:23:25,840 --> 00:23:28,560
But apparently it was enough to get some people interested.

374
00:23:28,960 --> 00:23:35,760
And I think one of the big different approaches that didn't necessarily it wasn't the UI design, I think, that was shining.

375
00:23:36,080 --> 00:23:39,120
I think I did some good UX to group some data together better.

376
00:23:40,080 --> 00:23:49,920
But one of the things I think that was really cool was just how you could interact with your day and the idea of having the map linked with the kind of data that you're trying to play back.

377
00:23:50,160 --> 00:23:54,720
So you could scroll through your day of runs and the map would update to show like what run you're looking at.

378
00:23:55,280 --> 00:23:58,560
But then on top of that, you could switch to a 3D mode.

379
00:23:58,960 --> 00:24:00,000
And I think that was the biggest.

380
00:24:01,760 --> 00:24:06,880
differentiator for slopes back then was the idea of, you know, skiers and snowboarders, we think in 3D.

381
00:24:07,200 --> 00:24:07,360
Right.

382
00:24:07,680 --> 00:24:08,640
You know, we have vertical.

383
00:24:08,880 --> 00:24:13,760
That's a variant that's just as important to us as where we go is like, well, what was that run like?

384
00:24:14,000 --> 00:24:14,560
Was it steep?

385
00:24:14,880 --> 00:24:15,680
Was it not steep?

386
00:24:16,480 --> 00:24:23,520
And so being able to 3D render a run, and back then this was pretty simple 3D rendering.

387
00:24:23,760 --> 00:24:24,960
I mean, we're talking iPhones.

388
00:24:25,200 --> 00:24:28,160
So I was going to say, was that that was there like towards the beginning of slopes?

389
00:24:28,320 --> 00:24:29,360
Yeah, that was in version one.

390
00:24:29,840 --> 00:24:30,400
Oh, wow.

391
00:24:30,480 --> 00:24:30,560
Okay.

392
00:24:30,960 --> 00:24:32,720
For some reason I thought that was something you added later.

393
00:24:33,200 --> 00:24:35,040
No, I did a big upgrade to it later.

394
00:24:36,000 --> 00:25:08,480
The one that shipped originally with Slopes was I taught myself OpenGL and all it basically was a simple 3D graph basically on a white or on a black background with a little checkerboard pattern floor so you had some depth perception and it would just do your speed as a color for like the tube of your track and the faster you went it would change more to green, slow would be red and then it would move like your little icon down there as it played back.

395
00:25:09,360 --> 00:25:11,520
So it wasn't like Google Earth or anything crazy.

396
00:25:12,080 --> 00:25:18,480
It was just a simple 3D rendering of the path that you took so you could see the horizontal and the vertical at the same time.

397
00:25:20,480 --> 00:25:36,000
I can tell you from experience, even though I have not used slopes because I haven't gone skiing in, I don't know, way too long, I have a friend at work who skies all the time and he recently found your app probably for my recommendation, I'm not sure.

398
00:25:36,160 --> 00:25:36,400
Thanks.

399
00:25:36,880 --> 00:25:44,000
And now every single time he comes back, the way he tells the story of all the things that happened is literally through the lens of your app.

400
00:25:44,240 --> 00:25:45,040
Oh, that's awesome.

401
00:25:45,200 --> 00:25:46,720
Because it's so visual.

402
00:25:48,000 --> 00:25:57,200
He'll be in the middle of telling a story and he'll pull it up as a way to sort of augment, like, yeah, we were trying to get across this ridge and we almost fell, but like, look at that, you know?

403
00:25:57,920 --> 00:26:02,640
And there's something extremely, like, it's not data you're looking at, it's a story.

404
00:26:03,040 --> 00:26:12,800
And I think that's why that 3D view, and now you have like the AR view and stuff, it really like resonates with the sort of after skiing viewing story.

405
00:26:13,440 --> 00:26:19,360
Yeah, that's that actually is really touching to hear because that's exactly my goal with slopes, as I always view it.

406
00:26:20,000 --> 00:26:32,480
Part of that startup weekend spiel that I gave was trying to say, like, I wanted an app that was more of a journal for your skiing and snowboarding experiences and less just like a bunch of data on the screen.

407
00:26:32,640 --> 00:26:51,680
And the visual is something I've really tried to hone in on, you know, what makes the most sense for skiers and snowboarders as we're trying to look back on our day and how can I augment that with things like photos or your friends or stuff like that and really get you like a holistic picture of what the story of your day was.

408
00:26:52,320 --> 00:27:00,400
Versus focusing on sort of the raw data training, which you could imagine going a very different route even with the exact same data.

409
00:27:00,400 --> 00:27:02,960
It's exactly the same data and like surfacing in a different way.

410
00:27:03,120 --> 00:27:05,680
Yours seems to be more focused on, like you said, the journaling.

411
00:27:05,840 --> 00:27:06,800
It's about telling a story.

412
00:27:07,680 --> 00:27:17,520
Yeah, like that 99 cent app, you know, like they'll have a bunch of graphs, like a 2D vertical graph that just shows like your speed versus your altitude and stuff like that.

413
00:27:17,600 --> 00:27:22,640
And like for an engineer that's trying to look into the data, that's the perfect kind of graph because that's just raw data.

414
00:27:22,960 --> 00:27:26,560
You can look at it, figure it out, but that doesn't tell a story.

415
00:27:27,040 --> 00:27:36,800
And that was the problem I was having with that other app that I felt I could do a better job than was that I could link all that same data together in a way that you could look back at it.

416
00:27:37,520 --> 00:27:38,880
and it would bring back memories.

417
00:27:39,040 --> 00:27:40,400
It wasn't just about analysis.

418
00:27:40,640 --> 00:27:46,480
It was about being able to look back in the middle of summer and being like, oh yeah, that time I went to Canada and had a lot of fun there.

419
00:27:46,640 --> 00:27:52,080
And you can go back really quick and look at what that trip actually was and not just like, oh okay, we're here.

420
00:27:52,240 --> 00:27:53,760
What's your top speed or something like that?

421
00:27:54,000 --> 00:27:54,480
Right, right.

422
00:27:54,960 --> 00:27:55,840
Yeah, that's awesome.

423
00:27:56,000 --> 00:27:56,800
And I don't know.

424
00:27:57,280 --> 00:28:02,160
It's cool because I've been listening to you talk about that app through your podcast Independence for a while.

425
00:28:02,640 --> 00:28:09,520
And then seeing someone basically do all of the exact things that you've talked about as your sort of pitch, it's, I don't know.

426
00:28:09,680 --> 00:28:11,280
It's really cool to actually see it in person.

427
00:28:11,840 --> 00:28:11,920
Yeah.

428
00:28:12,080 --> 00:28:17,280
Yeah, it's still something I think I've only seen it in the wild a couple of times.

429
00:28:19,040 --> 00:28:21,600
But at least I always hear from people like, oh, my friend uses it.

430
00:28:22,160 --> 00:28:29,120
And it's always warming to hear when people actually are using it the way you hope that you designed it for people to appreciate.

431
00:28:29,440 --> 00:28:36,160
Because there's always a difference between what you have in your mind and what you think you're actually coding and designing and doing everything.

432
00:28:37,120 --> 00:28:43,920
But the proof is really when you hear back and you know, you hear these stories being built around your app and how it's being used.

433
00:28:44,320 --> 00:28:48,240
And when those line up with what your original goals were, like that's one of the best feelings.

434
00:28:48,800 --> 00:28:49,440
It's really great.

435
00:28:50,240 --> 00:28:51,760
Yeah, that's awesome.

436
00:28:52,480 --> 00:28:56,000
So less warm and fuzzy and down to brass text then.

437
00:28:56,160 --> 00:29:05,120
Let's talk about business models because that's another area that Slopes has been a fascinating case study for me to see because you've gone through many of them and you talk openly about it.

438
00:29:06,480 --> 00:29:09,200
So starting out, what was the business model for Slopes?

439
00:29:10,320 --> 00:29:20,000
So I distinctly remember back at Startup Weekend, I was talking to a bunch of the mentors that were going around the room at the time, stuff like that.

440
00:29:20,640 --> 00:29:22,160
And Lauren Brichter was actually there.

441
00:29:22,640 --> 00:29:23,840
And he was one of the mentors.

442
00:29:24,160 --> 00:29:26,240
And like I was talking to him, big respect for him.

443
00:29:26,560 --> 00:29:26,640
Yeah.

444
00:29:27,200 --> 00:29:28,800
He was doing all that cool stuff back in the day.

445
00:29:28,960 --> 00:29:30,080
And like this was still that time.

446
00:29:30,480 --> 00:29:32,560
Like Tweety was still a thing.

447
00:29:33,120 --> 00:29:33,200
Yes.

448
00:29:33,680 --> 00:29:34,320
Rest in peace.

449
00:29:34,640 --> 00:29:37,680
Um, actually, I think by then Tweety had been sold.

450
00:29:38,240 --> 00:29:38,480
I think.

451
00:29:39,200 --> 00:29:39,280
Yeah.

452
00:29:39,600 --> 00:29:43,600
2013, I think it had been sold by, um, but still he was just coming off that.

453
00:29:43,760 --> 00:29:43,840
Yeah.

454
00:29:44,000 --> 00:29:44,080
Yeah.

455
00:29:44,480 --> 00:29:49,280
And, uh, I remember talking to him and he's like, oh, you know, what are you thinking for pricing and stuff like that?

456
00:29:49,360 --> 00:29:51,600
And this is when freemium was getting really popular.

457
00:29:52,000 --> 00:29:54,400
Like 99 cent rate to race to the bottom.

458
00:29:55,040 --> 00:29:55,680
This was that time.

459
00:29:56,320 --> 00:29:57,840
And I was all like, proud of myself.

460
00:29:58,000 --> 00:29:58,800
I'm like, you know what?

461
00:29:58,880 --> 00:30:01,200
I'm gonna, I'm not gonna racece to the bottom or anything.

462
00:30:01,840 --> 00:30:03,440
I'm going to charge real money for this app.

463
00:30:03,680 --> 00:30:05,120
I'm going to charge 499.

464
00:30:06,000 --> 00:30:08,560
And looking back, I'm just like, oh, that's so quaint.

465
00:30:09,440 --> 00:30:14,160
But I remember he looked at me and he's like, oh, double that price, double it.

466
00:30:15,760 --> 00:30:23,920
And I should have listened to him earlier, I was always afraid of trying to charge too much, and I stuck at 499 for quite some time.

467
00:30:24,640 --> 00:30:30,240
It wasn't until maybe the second year of Slopes, I started to experiment with the paid upfront price a little bit.

468
00:30:30,720 --> 00:30:34,800
I was always worried like people would notice if I changed the price or something, and nobody notices.

469
00:30:34,960 --> 00:30:35,040
Yeah.

470
00:30:35,520 --> 00:30:39,200
People care a lot less about you than you think they do.

471
00:30:39,360 --> 00:30:40,480
Yeah, nobody's going to remember.

472
00:30:40,560 --> 00:30:41,760
No friend's going to be like, What?

473
00:30:41,920 --> 00:30:43,200
I bought that app for $499.

474
00:30:43,360 --> 00:30:44,960
You're being ripped off for $799.

475
00:30:45,120 --> 00:30:46,160
No, nobody notices.

476
00:30:46,880 --> 00:30:56,720
But I did eventually raise the price to $799 and that worked pretty well in terms of like keeping the revenue a little bit higher than I was getting but not losing a bunch of users or downloads.

477
00:30:58,320 --> 00:31:05,760
But around that time, this is like early 2015, the second year, second winter of slopes having been completed.

478
00:31:06,960 --> 00:31:54,400
You know, I've been working on it kind of nights and weekends, taking a month off here and there maybe from consulting to work on it and I was kind of getting the itch to work on it more and more but the downloads weren't there and honestly I was getting tired of arguing with users like no no look trust me just pay eight dollars you'll love the app look here's why it's different like it's you can't make a sale like that it just it wasn't working um so I decided you know what I need to sit down and rethink this and figure out you know paid upfront is not working uh I refused to go 99 cent paid upfront and just try to make it up in volume because that I knew was not sustainable especially because I was starting to get server costs and everything from ongoing, like if in some of the other stuff I wanted to add would definitely add to that on a per user basis.

479
00:31:54,480 --> 00:31:56,400
So I'm like, I have to make up the money somehow.

480
00:31:57,600 --> 00:32:03,760
So recently I've been starting to call myself a subscription hipster because I was doing them before they were cool.

481
00:32:04,800 --> 00:32:22,160
So Slopes went subscription in 2015, which at the time was still when Apple was not letting users or letting developers use auto renew subscriptions for anything but dating apps or content apps or whatever the other two categories were.

482
00:32:23,520 --> 00:32:26,080
So I did non-auto renew yearly price.

483
00:32:26,480 --> 00:32:32,000
But I was looking around at people like Strava or Runkeeper, stuff like that, and they were doing a yearly price.

484
00:32:32,240 --> 00:32:38,160
And that was kind of my inspiration for, like, look, there are these other fitness apps out there that are doing some kind of yearly price.

485
00:32:39,440 --> 00:32:40,320
Why don't I try that?

486
00:32:40,640 --> 00:32:46,640
Like, that seems sustainable, that seems like if I get enough people, I'll be able to just, you know, keep building this up.

487
00:32:46,880 --> 00:32:50,480
And my goal is to keep people around and happy, which has always been my goal.

488
00:32:50,880 --> 00:32:53,440
So it aligned really well with my incentives, I thought.

489
00:32:54,080 --> 00:32:55,280
So I figured I'd try it out.

490
00:32:55,920 --> 00:33:00,320
And I just kind of settled on twenty dollars a year because like the running apps were.

491
00:33:00,480 --> 00:33:05,840
were anywhere from $40 to $50 a year, but you can't use slopes as much as a running app.

492
00:33:06,160 --> 00:33:08,480
So I felt like $20 felt about right.

493
00:33:09,360 --> 00:33:16,800
So that finally launched in 2015 and that was the first, I think, big business model change for slopes going subscription.

494
00:33:17,120 --> 00:33:31,360
And then quickly after that, once Apple released or approved, blessed the ability to use auto renewing subscriptions, I think it was like eight months after that, six months after that, I switched right away over to those, which was nice.

495
00:33:31,840 --> 00:33:36,880
But yeah, I was doing them back before like the official like floodgates opened on subscriptions.

496
00:33:36,880 --> 00:33:37,680
It's opened on subscriptions, I think.

497
00:33:38,720 --> 00:33:40,640
So how did you manage that transition?

498
00:33:41,040 --> 00:33:46,960
Because I think we've all seen bad transitions go down in many different ways.

499
00:33:47,840 --> 00:33:56,080
And they always result in people being very angry, both existing users, because that's a whole story that you have to figure out, and then people going forward hating them.

500
00:33:56,880 --> 00:33:58,320
So what was your approach to that?

501
00:33:59,360 --> 00:34:07,200
So fortunately back then, Slopes had only been downloaded, I think it was like 1800 times in two years.

502
00:34:08,720 --> 00:34:10,800
Like I had not made that much money with Slopes yet.

503
00:34:10,880 --> 00:34:12,160
I didn't get a ton of downloads.

504
00:34:13,120 --> 00:34:14,480
It was definitely slow going.

505
00:34:15,120 --> 00:34:27,760
So in terms of the number of people I had versus the size of my market, it was a sliver, which is great because that meant I didn't have to worry about, like, I could take care of these older users.

506
00:34:28,240 --> 00:34:36,480
I could bend over backwards for them because at the end of the day, they weren't going to be a huge drain on my resources because they were just a sliver of my market.

507
00:34:36,880 --> 00:34:37,120
Right.

508
00:34:37,760 --> 00:34:43,600
It wasn't like I had, you know, fifty percent of my market already acquired and now I have to figure out how to live with that.

509
00:34:44,720 --> 00:34:45,680
So I took good care of them.

510
00:34:45,920 --> 00:34:48,400
You know, I grandfathered everything in slow.

511
00:34:48,400 --> 00:34:58,880
In slopes, so slopes two is what launched the subscriptions and I grandfathered everything that they had in slopes one, they would get unlocked in slopes two.

512
00:34:59,600 --> 00:35:10,320
And there was like a receipt bug I had in the first point release, but most of them were pretty forgiving and that didn't really explode in my face, except for the bug.

513
00:35:11,040 --> 00:35:16,800
Most of them, I still have some of those people write in today and be like, hey, I've been using slopes since 2014 or something.

514
00:35:17,680 --> 00:35:19,440
It's like, hey, thanks for staying with me.

515
00:35:19,600 --> 00:35:20,480
Yeah, that's awesome.

516
00:35:20,960 --> 00:35:25,440
Yeah, it's really cool to see like they're still happy and some of them are still free users and that's fine.

517
00:35:26,960 --> 00:35:35,760
But so yeah, I made it a priority to really grandfather them and try and do right by them and not just be like, oh, you get a month free trial or something.

518
00:35:36,160 --> 00:35:37,760
Like, you paid me for features.

519
00:35:38,880 --> 00:35:50,000
I'm at a luxury right now since I have a small market share that I can just give you those features and I don't have to rely on milking you for money the same way that I might in another situation.

520
00:35:50,800 --> 00:35:54,480
So I did that and I tried to communicate early.

521
00:35:54,800 --> 00:35:56,000
Yeah, that was what I was going to say.

522
00:35:57,360 --> 00:36:01,760
Yeah, that was something that I tried my best to do.

523
00:36:02,480 --> 00:36:35,840
It was hard because I didn't have a huge email list back then or stuff like that, but I still tried to get the word out because I think looking at some of the transitions that have happened, even this year with some apps that have gone over to subscription models, one of the faults I saw was not communicating early enough and trying to make the big launch of the subscription version of the app like a big splash, like you would another point release back in the paid upgrade days where it's this big surprise and you're building towards it and then boom.

524
00:36:37,040 --> 00:36:40,480
Because if you rely on that, you're going to have a lot of people with misinformation.

525
00:36:41,200 --> 00:36:42,160
They're going to open the app.

526
00:36:42,880 --> 00:36:48,320
They're not really going to know what's going on because they're probably interacting it for the first time by opening it on their phone.

527
00:36:48,560 --> 00:36:48,720
Right.

528
00:36:48,880 --> 00:36:55,120
It's unlikely they read the Opus Mac Stories review that explains everything for you.

529
00:36:55,840 --> 00:36:56,160
Exactly.

530
00:36:56,960 --> 00:36:59,920
That's such a small sliver of the markets out there.

531
00:37:00,160 --> 00:37:01,840
Like most users don't do that stuff.

532
00:37:03,120 --> 00:37:09,680
So they're suddenly seeing these things and they're not sure if they have to pay for them or not, but people are probably going to assume the worst.

533
00:37:10,800 --> 00:37:12,480
And once you assume the worst, you have.

534
00:37:14,560 --> 00:37:52,240
lost adversion will kick in you know people get more worked up if they feel like you're taking away something from them than if you just never gave it to them so if they feel like you're removing features in any way that gets people worked up and then you'll have people spreading all that on twitter and i saw some of the people launching products this year like they're spending time on twitter triaging being like no no no no you get that for free you were a version x user version y you'll get that unlocked you're grandfathered but that lack of communication until launch day i feel like really kind of makes it hard for users to get the right information because it's all in the moment.

535
00:37:52,480 --> 00:37:54,640
Like you're having to fight those emotional reactions.

536
00:37:54,800 --> 00:37:59,200
You're not preparing them to say, hey, look, in a month, we're going subscription.

537
00:37:59,520 --> 00:38:00,640
You're an existing user.

538
00:38:00,960 --> 00:38:02,480
Here's how we're going to take care of you.

539
00:38:03,120 --> 00:38:15,040
And then launch day can be all focused on all those shiny new features that you're launching and all these awesome new things and not necessarily triage what you're going to do to grandfather people.

540
00:38:15,280 --> 00:38:15,440
Right.

541
00:38:15,680 --> 00:38:16,960
I think you really need to separate those.

542
00:38:17,520 --> 00:38:18,960
Otherwise, you'll get into trouble.

543
00:38:19,200 --> 00:38:19,760
Yeah, I agree.

544
00:38:20,000 --> 00:38:27,360
Especially because you will inevitably, I think literally every one these transitions I've seen had some sort of receipt related bugs.

545
00:38:27,760 --> 00:38:32,560
I don't know if that's something in Apple's system because I haven't done that yet or what, but that seems to always happen.

546
00:38:32,880 --> 00:38:39,920
And so you throw that into the mix where people are telling the truth when they're complaining and saying, I lost XYZ feature.

547
00:38:40,320 --> 00:38:43,680
And it's because, you know, some sort of day validation didn't happen right or whatever.

548
00:38:43,840 --> 00:38:50,480
And usually they get those fixed within a day because you're stressing out because you're getting, you know, destroyed on Twitter or whatever.

549
00:38:50,640 --> 00:38:54,160
But, but, but, but, that message is now out and seeped out.

550
00:38:54,320 --> 00:38:59,360
And a lot of people that's the last time they're going to hear about you is that story whenever that became a big wave.

551
00:38:59,680 --> 00:39:24,000
And so, yeah., I don't really have much room to talk because I've not done it myself, but I've always had the same feeling as what you're saying is that very early communication, specifically about the business model change and letting people know and air out all their grievances then takes a lot of the air out of that negativity for when you release and then the new information should be all the positive things, the new features, yada yada.

552
00:39:24,800 --> 00:39:25,600
Yeah, exactly.

553
00:39:26,160 --> 00:39:36,480
Yeah, it's not just communication on that too, I think is, I think that's one of the keys, but then I think the second key that a lot of people don't always sit down and think about is, you know.

554
00:39:36,720 --> 00:39:41,200
You know, what, how are you changing the product for those grandfathered users?

555
00:39:42,560 --> 00:39:57,280
So a really, I think, specific example would be how much are they going to be reminded that they're no longer the premium user and that you're trying to upsell to them throughout the app because they bought the app in a certain state.

556
00:39:57,680 --> 00:39:59,920
They bought the app and they...

557
00:40:00,960 --> 00:40:04,960
bought it without advertisements for your premium offering, whatever it is.

558
00:40:05,600 --> 00:40:18,320
And now they don't have the choice for if they're going to download a new version of your app, for example, that has these premium advertisements, you are now forcing that on them.

559
00:40:18,560 --> 00:40:21,200
You've taken the control out of their hands.

560
00:40:21,760 --> 00:40:26,880
And anytime you take control out of somebody's hands, that will really anger them.

561
00:40:27,200 --> 00:40:29,680
People do not like feeling forced into anything.

562
00:40:30,480 --> 00:40:40,720
So you really have to sit down and consider the psychology of the user and look at, okay, what did they have before and how will that change today?

563
00:40:41,040 --> 00:40:43,280
What am I going to be putting in their way?

564
00:40:43,840 --> 00:40:47,200
You know, what am I reminding them of all the time that has changed?

565
00:40:48,080 --> 00:40:52,320
And you kinda have to really think that through, I think.

566
00:40:52,560 --> 00:41:00,400
And you have to just think more about how users will emotionally respond from a base level, like loss aversion.

567
00:41:00,960 --> 00:41:02,080
You really have to think that through.

568
00:41:02,480 --> 00:41:10,240
And it's easy as a product owner to look at it and be like, oh, you know, whatever, it's just a small badge here on this screen and that screen and like whatever.

569
00:41:11,520 --> 00:41:16,720
But I think we underestimate emotional responses of users a lot.

570
00:41:16,960 --> 00:41:18,720
And that's something you have to have empathy for.

571
00:41:19,440 --> 00:41:27,680
Because at the end of the day, if you don't have that empathy for how they might receive something, that's going to affect your your business and that's not good.

572
00:41:28,560 --> 00:41:38,480
Yeah, like that feeling of hitting a paywall, especially if there's a big shiny badge that's telling you you have to hit this button, and then you hit it and then it gives you a paywall.

573
00:41:38,880 --> 00:41:43,200
That's a negative feeling that you're going to get pretty much 100% of the time, I think.

574
00:41:44,240 --> 00:41:52,800
You might then rationalize it, internalize it, decide to buy it, decide not to, whatever, but that feeling is going to be negative and you're kind of evoking that in all your users.

575
00:41:53,120 --> 00:41:56,720
And that wasn't an experience they had the day before that update came out.

576
00:41:57,520 --> 00:42:00,160
If, if, you know, it was a paid up front app before that.

577
00:42:01,120 --> 00:42:08,880
Right, and that's not something that they had kind of mentally reconciled when they paid those nine dollars or whatever they paid upfront for your app.

578
00:42:09,840 --> 00:42:13,680
They hadn't had to sit down and choose, like, Oh, well, this thing has ads.

579
00:42:14,000 --> 00:42:15,520
It's still worth nine dollars to me.

580
00:42:15,920 --> 00:42:18,640
Like they had bought it because it didn't have ads.

581
00:42:18,800 --> 00:42:19,680
Right, and now it will.

582
00:42:19,920 --> 00:42:25,680
It's especially true for apps that are part of their main selling point is like a really clean design.

583
00:42:26,160 --> 00:42:26,240
Yeah.

584
00:42:27,120 --> 00:42:36,800
Yeah, I think too, there's this thing where, you know, if you bought an app that was paid upfront, you were the target audience for that probably, right?

585
00:42:37,600 --> 00:42:45,840
And then whenever something switches over, like especially if that paid-out front model fell apart, you stopped updating the app because it didn't make financial sense.

586
00:42:46,000 --> 00:42:48,480
It was costing you more money than you were making or whatever.

587
00:42:48,800 --> 00:42:51,280
And then you decide, you know what, I'm going to resurrect this app.

588
00:42:52,240 --> 00:42:56,160
It's going to be better for everybody because at least there will be updates going forward.

589
00:42:56,320 --> 00:43:03,120
I'm not going to take any features away, whatever, but I'm going to add all these new features and it's behind a paywall that's now subscription-based.

590
00:43:03,680 --> 00:43:12,480
But for all those users, they went from when they bought the app, and maybe they're still regular users, they were the target audience to suddenly you're switching your focus to a new...

591
00:43:12,480 --> 00:43:17,360
A new audience that's willing to pay more for more professional features or whatever it be.

592
00:43:17,840 --> 00:43:26,080
But that's also kind of a negative feeling is to have the focus of the designers and developers of the app be on you and then shift it away from you.

593
00:43:26,640 --> 00:43:29,840
I feel like we're almost talking about what's happened with Apple with the past ten years.

594
00:43:30,080 --> 00:43:47,600
I feel like a lot of people, especially if you look at the Mac Pro blowback, the older Mac Pro or even the new one to some degree, for a long time, people who were developers really felt like Apple was targeting them before the iPhone.

595
00:43:48,160 --> 00:43:53,280
You know, they, you know, I had a G5, uh, Mac because I needed that power.

596
00:43:53,600 --> 00:43:56,080
I was doing some serious programming and that's what I needed.

597
00:43:56,400 --> 00:44:00,080
And like, it really felt like Apple was targeting me with that machine.

598
00:44:00,560 --> 00:44:07,920
And I think ever since the iPhone has come out and they're working on the watch and like their market has opened up a lot wider.

599
00:44:08,560 --> 00:44:16,880
And I think some of the negativity we see regarding Apple and what they're coming out with is not necessarily that they're coming out with bad products.

600
00:44:17,200 --> 00:44:18,960
It's that people feel left behind.

601
00:44:19,280 --> 00:44:20,960
Like they're no longer that target market.

602
00:44:21,200 --> 00:44:23,040
And you're really hitting the nail on the head there with that.

603
00:44:24,240 --> 00:44:31,280
That that really triggers something in people that they can take a company that they absolutely love or take a product or an app that they love.

604
00:44:31,920 --> 00:44:35,360
And if you feel left behind, you're going to hate it.

605
00:44:35,760 --> 00:44:46,400
It maybe not right away, but slowly over time as things keep moving, like it can be a wonderful, amazing product still, but you're going to start hating it more and more because it's not your product anymore.

606
00:44:46,640 --> 00:44:47,600
It's not made for you.

607
00:44:48,320 --> 00:44:50,000
It's made for them, someone else.

608
00:44:50,240 --> 00:44:51,600
And that, that hits hard, I think.

609
00:44:51,920 --> 00:44:52,000
Yeah.

610
00:44:52,400 --> 00:45:02,160
Like if you have an intensely focused company that doesn't do everything but they're focused on you and doting on you constantly with new wonderful magical toys.

611
00:45:02,560 --> 00:45:03,120
That's amazing.

612
00:45:03,280 --> 00:45:08,560
But the moment it slightly shifts away from you, you can feel it moving away and like there's nothing you can do to get it back.

613
00:45:08,720 --> 00:45:10,560
It's not like there's another company doing the same thing.

614
00:45:10,960 --> 00:45:12,640
And so yeah, yeah, that's interesting.

615
00:45:12,720 --> 00:45:25,360
And I guess that applies in the inverse going from paid upfront to subscription because often, not always, but often you're switching your focus from a more mass market, you're going for scale, right?

616
00:45:25,920 --> 00:45:32,320
To I don't need as many people, but I can monetize those people more and I can give them better features and spend more time on this.

617
00:45:32,560 --> 00:46:28,480
But you're shifting your focus from mass market to a more focused you know business oriented market and it's like the inverse of what you're talking about with apple and in either one of those cases yeah how do you communicate and keep the other people happy while sort of shifting your your eye to somebody else and maybe you just can't yeah i mean honestly that's sometimes you can't and that's okay i think you know you're always gonna have criticisms when you switch business models you're always gonna have people that don't like the change and you're always gonna be alienating some users like they are no longer the target in some way and i think to some degree As long as you're confident in the direction you're going, you just have to stay the course and just accept the fact that, yes, I will piss off some of my existing users, but at the end of the day, this is the better direction for that company, for my product.

618
00:46:29,520 --> 00:46:37,600
Yeah, and I think that's the trap that a lot of us, me highly included, fall into is being afraid of that vocal angry base.

619
00:46:38,400 --> 00:46:48,400
Even if you've even if you've sat down and you've really thought through everything and you think long term, this is the best way to go, that fear of that hit of negativity that you know you're going to get might.

620
00:46:48,560 --> 00:46:51,520
might freeze you from making the better decision for everybody.

621
00:46:52,480 --> 00:46:54,560
And that's that's not really a good place to be either.

622
00:46:55,040 --> 00:46:55,360
Oh, no.

623
00:46:55,520 --> 00:46:55,600
Yeah.

624
00:46:55,840 --> 00:46:58,160
It's it's a terrible place to be as a product owner.

625
00:46:58,320 --> 00:47:08,000
You don't if you're anything like me, you know, your product is somehow tied to your self identity or even your self worth and it can be really hard to receive that negative criticism.

626
00:47:08,800 --> 00:47:16,800
See many episodes of Independence in which I go on and on about the emotional turmoil I'm in dealing with customer support and all that kind of stuff.

627
00:47:18,160 --> 00:47:23,040
It can be hard to just accept the fact that you're going to get that negative criticism and you just kind of have to live with it.

628
00:47:23,840 --> 00:47:28,640
But you do have to look at it kind of objectively and figure out, is there too much criticism here?

629
00:47:29,040 --> 00:47:29,760
Did I miss the mark?

630
00:47:31,760 --> 00:47:36,080
Or is this just the criticism that should be expected with this kind of transition?

631
00:47:36,400 --> 00:47:36,560
Right.

632
00:47:36,800 --> 00:47:38,480
Yeah, because it's really a broader story.

633
00:47:38,640 --> 00:47:43,440
It's someone who doesn't like a business model generally and they're taking it on you because you're the latest example.

634
00:47:43,760 --> 00:47:44,480
But like, right.

635
00:47:44,960 --> 00:47:46,800
There's nothing you could have done that would have made them happy.

636
00:47:46,880 --> 00:47:46,960
Yeah.

637
00:47:47,440 --> 00:47:47,520
Yeah.

638
00:47:47,920 --> 00:47:55,280
It doesn't help either that I get a lot of emails that's like, man, I really appreciate you having just a paid up front app without subscriptions.

639
00:47:55,360 --> 00:47:56,160
I hate subscriptions.

640
00:47:56,560 --> 00:47:58,240
You're amazing for doing this exact thing.

641
00:47:58,720 --> 00:48:06,080
And it like, it's not stated, but it's it's implied or at least I'm reading into it that it's like if you ever switch, I will turn against you.

642
00:48:06,320 --> 00:48:09,040
And like, every one of those actually gives me this pain.

643
00:48:09,600 --> 00:48:10,800
I will burn your world down.

644
00:48:12,160 --> 00:48:12,240
Yeah.

645
00:48:12,480 --> 00:48:20,160
That's like, it literally is like, it's both very kind and also like, has this tinge of like, I'm afraid of you now.

646
00:48:21,920 --> 00:48:22,000
Yeah.

647
00:48:22,320 --> 00:48:22,400
Yeah.

648
00:48:22,640 --> 00:48:25,440
It's those people are very, very vocal.

649
00:48:26,080 --> 00:48:27,760
Um, fortunately, I don't know.

650
00:48:27,920 --> 00:48:29,520
I feel like I don't get a lot of them.

651
00:48:30,400 --> 00:48:33,280
Um, I don't know what the secret sauce there is.

652
00:48:33,360 --> 00:48:42,880
I mean, I do get some app reviews being like, oh, I love it if it would be paid upfront or a one-time payment thing, but it's like five stars great features wish I only had to pay once.

653
00:48:43,440 --> 00:48:47,600
Like I don't know how I'm not getting absolutely raked over the coals for being subscriptions.

654
00:48:48,240 --> 00:48:55,920
I do think maybe maybe being clearly kind of more of an independent app and not of a big company kind of helps.

655
00:48:57,040 --> 00:48:58,240
I know that helps me in some ways.

656
00:48:58,320 --> 00:49:02,480
I'm not 100% sure if that helps me get people less pissed off at me in this way.

657
00:49:03,920 --> 00:49:10,640
But somehow Slopes has largely avoided angry criticism about that.

658
00:49:10,880 --> 00:49:12,640
You know, I'll have some complaints, but certainly no one.

659
00:49:13,280 --> 00:49:15,120
angry that I'm subscription or something like that.

660
00:49:15,760 --> 00:49:18,000
Probably they just delete the app and say screw it.

661
00:49:18,800 --> 00:49:20,000
But what can you do?

662
00:49:20,320 --> 00:49:24,880
Yeah, I think probably it's the transition where you get most of that sort of vitriol.

663
00:49:25,600 --> 00:49:33,760
And if it wasn't a giant user base before you made that transition and you handled that transition well, you're probably okay then after that.

664
00:49:34,000 --> 00:49:36,160
See, this is why it's good to be a subscription hipster.

665
00:49:36,320 --> 00:49:36,400
Yeah.

666
00:49:36,960 --> 00:49:38,000
Before there are a lot of users.

667
00:49:39,760 --> 00:49:47,680
Okay, so before I let you go, I do want to ask about Android because I know that's a thing you're sort of working through right now and maybe there's a little...

668
00:49:48,240 --> 00:50:25,760
uh not drama but it's a it's a struggle and it's a thing that i think a lot of iOS developers just never even try to dip their toes into um but you are or at least you were as less i checked so how is that going still am oh boy um it's i don't know some turmoil um it's just hard to find uh you know well i any industry it can be hard to find reliable good contractors um i don't want to go with an agency i'd rather have some kind of long-term partnership with a contractor or maybe someone i eventu I eventually bring on board.

669
00:50:26,240 --> 00:50:33,440
But agencies can cost a lot more money because they have to worry about keeping everyone on their bench paid and like all the stuff that causes overhead there.

670
00:50:34,720 --> 00:50:39,840
So I'm trying to find an independent contractor and that's challenging in any industry.

671
00:50:40,480 --> 00:50:53,360
And I feel like, and this might be a very unfair thing to say, but I feel like Android, it's especially more challenging because my theory is a bunch of companies just try to check that box.

672
00:50:53,760 --> 00:50:55,200
You know, they just need the Android app.

673
00:50:55,520 --> 00:50:57,360
Not to say there are no good Android apps.

674
00:50:57,680 --> 00:50:58,320
There certainly are.

675
00:50:59,040 --> 00:51:00,560
But so many companies.

676
00:51:01,200 --> 00:51:04,080
are just built around, look, we need to finally ship an Android app.

677
00:51:04,320 --> 00:51:20,720
Let's get it out the door that the people that contract for Android don't have the same level of polish and attention to detail and everything that you get with a lot of Apple apps on our platform and we have plenty of apps, don't get me wrong.

678
00:51:21,360 --> 00:51:33,680
There are plenty of terrible apps out there, but the market maybe shapes contractors more towards like efficiency and speed slash cost versus high polish.

679
00:51:34,240 --> 00:51:34,560
Exactly.

680
00:51:34,720 --> 00:51:50,000
For whatever reason that is, how the store is designed, the way that Google has monetized or set up the Play Store, whatever the reason is, which I definitely am too ignorant to make a good call on, maybe that's like part of that is the market sort of shapes that.

681
00:51:50,400 --> 00:51:50,480
Yeah.

682
00:51:50,720 --> 00:52:09,920
And I think there's also a smaller market for Android apps because of the monetization problems because there are a lot of downloads for Android apps, but there are people are a lot less willing to pay on Android because they get the free phone or whatever the reason is, a lot less people pay for in-app purchases or whatever on Android.

683
00:52:10,480 --> 00:52:17,840
So you also then have a smaller market potential pool of contractors to support that because the market isn't huge.

684
00:52:18,400 --> 00:52:28,640
And when people are willing to pay for those, uh, to pay to have that app written, they probably invest less money because they don't expect a large return.

685
00:52:28,960 --> 00:52:33,600
Like I'm expecting IOS is going to be paying for my Android contractor for quite some time.

686
00:52:34,160 --> 00:52:36,240
I'm not going to make that money up on Android.

687
00:52:36,400 --> 00:52:37,920
I'm not going to break even any time soon.

688
00:52:38,080 --> 00:52:40,320
This is a sump cost and I'm okay with that.

689
00:52:40,720 --> 00:52:46,800
But that means that a lot of the people that I'm looking at right now, like a bunch of people that emailed me are part-time contractors.

690
00:52:46,960 --> 00:52:50,720
They have a full-time gig and they'll do Android on the side to earn some extra money or something.

691
00:52:51,440 --> 00:53:01,040
It's not nearly the flood of people I feel like I find in the IOS world where they're full time contractors, doing a bunch of gigs or something like that.

692
00:53:02,160 --> 00:53:08,880
It seems a lot more side project and I've gotten some full time people and the guy I was previously working with was full time on it.

693
00:53:09,600 --> 00:53:14,880
But it's, I think, just been harder for me to find someone that's good to work with.

694
00:53:15,760 --> 00:54:06,960
And yeah, so that's kind of been the drama as of late is just having to say goodbye to one contractor and no big drama there just you know we had to part ways some life issues came up on his end and just couldn't support the contract anymore so trying to find someone new and you know it kind of knocks the wind out of your sail having it be like okay here's this thing I'm working on and I made the decision to work on it last August and like started paying money real money towards this and now I kind of feel like I'm back to square one like I have a code base but you know I have to find somebody again I have to go through all that so yeah a long story there but it's it's going I am still actively working on the Android project it's just kind of the wind got knocked out of my sails and trying to find someone else this week and keep moving forward.

695
00:54:07,440 --> 00:54:09,920
So how are you approaching that app then?

696
00:54:10,160 --> 00:54:14,400
Is the idea, like, do you want it to be a consistent experience across both?

697
00:54:14,720 --> 00:54:29,760
Or are you kind of thinking, take the, like, sort of ideals and principles of slopes and translate that into what a proper native Android app would be, which might look different, maybe even radically, than what you currently have?

698
00:54:30,400 --> 00:54:31,360
Yeah, very much the second.

699
00:54:32,560 --> 00:54:36,640
I mean, I think what has made slopes, or at least what I hear really makes slopes.

700
00:54:37,280 --> 00:55:26,640
great for users is that you know some people will say it's like the ski app that apple never made like i really try and be a good platform app i try and fit in on the system and i want to do the same thing over on android you know i feel like all too often over there they are used to being second class citizens you know they get the apps they get the checked box oh look here's your android app and i don't want to do that i want to feel like a proper google app as much as i can so i feel like i'll pull a lot of similarities for like how i think about data and how i visualize it and maybe the map screen will be very much the same but it's going to be like using their material theme and it's not going to feel like it's just a React native port over and it's the same looking UI on both platforms that don't feel at home really on either platform.

701
00:55:27,600 --> 00:55:29,920
It's very much going to be an Android app.

702
00:55:31,280 --> 00:55:32,240
So how are you doing that?

703
00:55:32,720 --> 00:55:34,880
Like, are you leading the design there?

704
00:55:35,360 --> 00:55:42,000
Or are you planning on, I mean, obviously you would lean on your contractor, but like, would that also come into play on the design side?

705
00:55:42,240 --> 00:55:46,960
Or are you kind of buying a bunch of Android phones and living the Android lifestyle a little bit to kind of understand that?

706
00:55:47,360 --> 00:55:48,000
No, so yeah.

707
00:55:48,000 --> 00:55:53,680
Yeah, I have a Pixel 3a that I bought back in last winter and I've been, I wouldn't say living on it.

708
00:55:53,840 --> 00:56:01,200
I'm not a savage, but I am definitely using it and perusing apps and I've gotten some recommendations and actually found some really good apps over there.

709
00:56:01,520 --> 00:56:04,160
Wikipedia, by the way, has an amazing Android app.

710
00:56:04,320 --> 00:56:05,120
They do a really good job.

711
00:56:05,520 --> 00:56:05,760
Really?

712
00:56:06,080 --> 00:56:08,080
Yeah, there are definitely a couple of others.

713
00:56:08,160 --> 00:56:11,680
So I've been actually, that's a thing that I've been looking for.

714
00:56:11,840 --> 00:56:13,600
Like, for me, there's Pocketcast.

715
00:56:13,920 --> 00:56:22,000
That's kind of like the perennial, amazing Android app, which is also on iOS, but kind of the example, the one that my friends are like, oh yeah, we pay for apps.

716
00:56:22,000 --> 00:56:22,880
I pay for Pocketcast.

717
00:56:23,680 --> 00:56:46,560
But I don't know, like, I don't know if it's what we were talking about earlier, like the actual market just doesn't exist the same way, or if it's because I don't run in the same circles, but I don't know who the sort of stars of the Android indie app development scene are, or who those, what those apps are, because I've selfishly kind of looked for the show, because I don't want this to just be an iOS show.

718
00:56:46,880 --> 00:56:50,800
And I've kind of struggled to find who those darlings are.

719
00:56:51,360 --> 00:56:56,160
Yeah, I don't know how much the indie lifestyle is over there.

720
00:56:57,280 --> 00:57:02,080
Again, I feel like it's harder to earn a living over there because less people are willing to pay, you have all that.

721
00:57:02,560 --> 00:57:07,280
Even Pocketcasts, you know, they were what a three-person team when they launched their Android app, somewhere around there, I think.

722
00:57:08,240 --> 00:57:10,400
And now they're, you know, company working for what?

723
00:57:10,640 --> 00:57:11,600
NPR or whatever.

724
00:57:12,320 --> 00:57:21,840
Or whatever that conglomeration of things is that bought Pocketcasts, and I feel like I do see a lot less independent people over there.

725
00:57:22,320 --> 00:57:30,720
And even when I was reaching out to some people I know at Google on the material team on Twitter, just being like, hey, what are your favorite material apps?

726
00:57:33,040 --> 00:57:36,000
I got one person reply with like three or four, maybe.

727
00:57:36,720 --> 00:57:41,520
And I expected like if you're on iOS, you can surf through the app store and find a bunch of well designed apps.

728
00:57:41,760 --> 00:57:48,640
Yeah, and if you ask anyone, they'll go through like their favorites and pitch you on it basically because you kind of have this relationship.

