00:58:10,480 --> 00:58:17,200
Something like that, yeah., it's been hard to find good apps over there.

736
00:58:17,600 --> 00:58:21,600
Again, not to say there aren't any, but it has been a bit harder.

737
00:58:21,840 --> 00:58:24,000
Like I feel like, or some of the other ones.

738
00:58:24,160 --> 00:58:26,000
National Geographic, I think is pretty good.

739
00:58:26,240 --> 00:58:27,760
Starbucks actually is a okay one.

740
00:58:28,480 --> 00:58:30,160
Wikipedia, a couple more.

741
00:58:30,640 --> 00:58:39,600
But yeah, they're just, I feel like Google's struggle to get people to adopt their stuff, like material design and stuff like that.

742
00:58:40,800 --> 00:58:42,480
I mean, that's a whole Google conversation.

743
00:58:43,040 --> 00:58:54,960
But it's not like IOS where when they came out with, you know, the idea of the card UI a couple iOSs ago, everybody is suddenly using that or iOS 7 with a visual effect view.

744
00:58:55,520 --> 00:59:01,440
We had what was it, FX blur view back then, a third-party implementation of it because we didn't have official system support yet.

745
00:59:01,680 --> 00:59:04,640
And like all the apps were using that third-party plugin on day one.

746
00:59:05,200 --> 00:59:07,600
A lot of people try and fit in with the system and adapt to it.

747
00:59:08,160 --> 00:59:14,880
But I feel like Google apps kind of just get stuck in whatever generation of Android they wrote.

748
00:59:15,280 --> 00:59:23,520
So if you look back, I can look at the Strava app and be like, oh, okay, they were written during this Android because they're using these ideas and they never updated from that.

749
00:59:24,320 --> 00:59:26,080
And I feel like that's just kind of what happens.

750
00:59:26,240 --> 00:59:28,640
Things get launched and kind of get stagnant over there.

751
00:59:29,120 --> 00:59:33,280
So it's harder to find these up-to-date examples of, oh, here are some great apps.

752
00:59:33,760 --> 00:59:35,520
Uh, you know, here's my 30 best apps.

753
00:59:35,680 --> 00:59:39,760
You know, I probably have four or five on my pixel that I'm like, okay, these are really good.

754
00:59:40,800 --> 00:59:40,800
Yeah.

755
00:59:42,000 --> 00:59:53,920
It's interesting and it's weird too because like Google, like you said, when they launched their Material Design spec, uh, it was pretty well received and, and well, like used.

756
00:59:54,240 --> 00:59:55,120
Like you see it everywhere.

757
00:59:55,280 --> 00:59:57,600
It's almost like the new, uh, oh shoot.

758
00:59:57,680 --> 00:59:58,240
What is it called?

759
00:59:58,400 --> 00:59:58,960
The bootstrap.

760
00:59:59,040 --> 00:59:59,600
It's almost like.

761
01:00:00,080 --> 01:00:01,120
the name bootstrap, right?

762
01:00:01,280 --> 01:00:15,520
Like if you're going to make like a basic little React web app for some system that you're going to use internally, you don't plan to release it, you're just going to throw a material design on there because it's easy, it looks generally nice, and it's a consistent thing that people understand.

763
01:00:15,840 --> 01:00:17,680
And I do feel that on Android.

764
01:00:17,840 --> 01:00:33,600
Like when I, because I was an Android user for pretty long time, even up till that came out, and that was a selling feature, the same way that we say, you know, iOS 13, well designed, like follows Apple patterns, whatever people would say, this is a material designed app, and that was a selling point.

765
01:00:33,840 --> 01:00:39,360
So it's not that design isn't cared about or focused over there, but it, yeah, it does seem like there's.

766
01:00:39,360 --> 01:00:44,240
It seems like there's, I don't know, there's something, maybe it could literally be the difference between the stores.

767
01:00:44,400 --> 01:00:50,000
Like the editorial team at Apple might be really good at surfacing, you know, those types of apps.

768
01:00:50,240 --> 01:00:55,200
And then when those are successful business wise, they stay around and they stay updated.

769
01:00:55,760 --> 01:00:59,920
And then that inspires other people to do the same thing and it kind of creates a culture of design.

770
01:01:00,480 --> 01:01:02,080
And maybe that part doesn't exist over there.

771
01:01:02,240 --> 01:01:04,720
I guess there's a million vectors and it's impossible to really know.

772
01:01:05,200 --> 01:01:16,320
When you also have people who, you know, you have material theming come out and I forget how far back you can backport it to, but you have people that are running five-year-old phones, four-year-old phones that can't get the latest operating system.

773
01:01:16,800 --> 01:01:18,560
So anything that Google comes out with, you know, we.

774
01:01:18,960 --> 01:01:25,200
We joke in the iOS community, iOS 14 is going to be announced in what, 28 days or something like that.

775
01:01:25,680 --> 01:01:29,200
And we're like, oh good, we can finally use iOS 13 features.

776
01:01:29,440 --> 01:01:33,440
Like, yay, it's time to start using that stuff because we can drop support for iOS 12.

777
01:01:34,560 --> 01:01:36,880
Android has a much longer horizon there.

778
01:01:37,360 --> 01:01:50,640
So unless you have something like a polyfill library that'll let you backport all that functionality to older systems, it is a lot harder to jump on whatever the latest and greatest initiative is because you have to support all those older phones.

779
01:01:50,800 --> 01:01:51,840
Yeah, that makes sense.

780
01:01:53,200 --> 01:01:53,280
Yeah.

781
01:01:53,760 --> 01:01:55,840
But yeah, I'm sure it's a multitude of reasons.

782
01:01:56,080 --> 01:01:57,600
But yeah, I mean, they're good.

783
01:01:57,600 --> 01:02:02,640
Yeah, I mean, there are good ones out there and that's my hope is that Slopes will be a good Android app.

784
01:02:02,880 --> 01:02:09,680
That's really my goal for it is, you know, I wanted to be like when Pocketcast came out and the Android community was like, Oh, this is an amazing app.

785
01:02:10,000 --> 01:02:12,640
Like, this feels just as good as an iOS app.

786
01:02:13,040 --> 01:02:13,680
And rewarded it.

787
01:02:14,160 --> 01:02:14,880
Yeah, they did.

788
01:02:15,840 --> 01:02:20,400
And now I think Pocketcast was a bit of a good target market for Android users.

789
01:02:21,680 --> 01:02:30,480
That's a whole different thing, but they did definitely come out with a good design and I think that plus the market ended up rewarding them pretty nicely.

790
01:02:31,120 --> 01:02:34,720
So I'd hope to be that whenever I launch.

791
01:02:35,760 --> 01:02:35,840
Yeah.

792
01:02:36,160 --> 01:02:36,240
Yeah.

793
01:02:36,240 --> 01:02:42,480
Yeah, well, interestingly, my friend who's the one who uses your app all the time, he was an Android user.

794
01:02:42,640 --> 01:02:46,560
And whenever he switched over to the iPhone was when he got your app because he had already known about it.

795
01:02:46,960 --> 01:03:02,480
But he was a person, and I know he knows people that want it for Android specifically because they go in big groups of people and they like sharing all that data and being able to, like it's a selling point even for IOS people that their friends can get the app.

796
01:03:02,880 --> 01:03:11,760
Even if you're not making all the money on the Android side, it's still a feature for iPhone people just because of that social aspect of it, I guess.

797
01:03:12,160 --> 01:03:14,880
Yeah, that's exactly why I finally committed to doing an Android app.

798
01:03:15,120 --> 01:04:00,960
app was I realized that the iOS experience was starting to become hindered because I didn't have an Android counterpart because I mean let's be honest you know you me our Twitter friends like we probably all run iOS we're in that community but if you look at families or you look at group of friends almost no group is going to have a single operating system it's going to be a mix and when I launched some of the social features last year where like when you're looking back on your day if you have a friend and you've agreed to location share within slopes you can see your friends moving around the map at the same resort that you were at with them So if they went off and did a couple runs somewhere else, you'll see their avatar moving around the map over there while you're over on your stuff.

799
01:04:01,360 --> 01:04:08,080
And like that kind of stuff you can't do without an Android wrap because half their friends don't show up on the map anymore.

